# Directly standardised mortality rates

This lesson is a one-to-one port of the preparation and point-estimate portion of
the EpiR Handbook standardisation chapter. It uses the same five source tables,
the same age-and-sex strata, and the same World Standard Population weights.

The selected source scope contains no figure. Confidence intervals are outside
this first port because the source delegates them to a separate package routine.

```biolang
let demographics_a = read_csv("country_demographics.csv")
let demographics_b = read_csv("country_demographics_2.csv", {sep: ";"})
let deaths_a = read_csv("deaths_countryA.csv")
let deaths_b = read_csv("deaths_countryB.csv")
let standard = read_csv("world_standard_population_by_sex.csv")

fn country_rows(country, demographics, deaths, standard_population) {
  return range(0, nrow(demographics))
    |> flat_map(|i| [
      {
        Country: country,
        age_cat5: demographics[i].age_cat5,
        Sex: "Male",
        Population: demographics[i].m,
        Deaths: deaths[i].Male,
        standard_population: standard_population[i * 2].WorldStandardPopulation
      },
      {
        Country: country,
        age_cat5: demographics[i].age_cat5,
        Sex: "Female",
        Population: demographics[i].f,
        Deaths: deaths[i].Female,
        standard_population: standard_population[i * 2 + 1].WorldStandardPopulation
      }
    ])
}

let standardised = table(concat(
  country_rows("A", demographics_a, deaths_a, standard),
  country_rows("B", demographics_b, deaths_b, standard)
))
  |> group_by("Country")
  |> summarize(|country, rows| {
    let weighted_deaths = rows
      |> map(|row| row.Deaths * 1.0 / row.Population * row.standard_population)
      |> sum()
    let standard_total = rows |> col("standard_population") |> sum()
    return {
      Country: country,
      deaths: rows |> col("Deaths") |> sum(),
      population: rows |> col("Population") |> sum(),
      standard_population: standard_total,
      rate_per_100000: weighted_deaths / standard_total * 100000.0
    }
  })
  |> arrange("Country")

print(standardised)
```

The directly standardised rate is a weighted average of stratum-specific rates.
Using one common reference population makes the two countries comparable even
when their age and sex structures differ.

```biolang
assert standardised[0].Country == "A", "first country is A"
assert abs(standardised[0].rate_per_100000 - 23.56685849327109) < 1e-10, "country A directly standardised rate"
assert standardised[1].Country == "B", "second country is B"
assert abs(standardised[1].rate_per_100000 - 19.32549423719546) < 1e-10, "country B directly standardised rate"
```
